what_is_stored:
Agor's own full copy of each agent conversation, one database row per message. A message row holds type, role, index, timestamp, a 200-character preview, parent_tool_use_id, and a JSON `data` blob with content, tool_uses and metadata. Around the messages it stores:
- sessions, with agentic_tool (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, Copilot, OpenCode, Cursor beta), sdk_session_id for continuity with the vendor SDK, and genealogy (parent_session_id, forked_from_session_id, spawn_point_task_id/message_index);
- tasks, the units of work inside a session, each with a message_range and git_state;
- branches (worktrees or clones), repos, boards, zones, cards, board comments, schedules, users, groups and RBAC grants;
- MCP servers with their OAuth tokens, encrypted user API keys, uploads, artifacts, and gateway (Slack/Discord) channel and thread mappings;
- a knowledge base of markdown documents with immutable versions, plus embeddings and graph nodes.

Teammates are persistent agents that keep MEMORY.md and SOUL.md files. The code itself lives in git worktrees, not in the database. Sources: schema.sqlite.ts, https://agor.live/llms-full.txt, https://agor.live/guide/knowledge

lesson_for_parinn:
Agor shows that team-wide, real-time sharing of agent sessions does not need git as the transport. Its approach is one trusted writer that owns the database, plus untrusted executors that hold no database access and send events through scoped, short-lived credentials. Cross-replica coordination uses database leases and fences, and the Redis pub/sub layer is explicitly not the log of record. ParleyInn could take the same split:
- the agent-side hook stays network-free and only appends to its local hash-chained log;
- a separate, optional relay or sync component does any fan-out;
- the record of truth stays outside the fan-out layer.

Agor also shows what ParleyInn should not copy. Its transcripts are mutable JSON rows that can be patched and are deleted by cascade, with no chaining, no redaction and no enveloping of recalled content. That leaves the "auditable canvas" claim on weaker ground than ParleyInn's I2, I6 and I10 invariants. Two pieces are worth adopting directly:
- the task-level git_state (sha_at_start and sha_at_end per message range) as a cheap way to link sessions to code;
- the deterministic, SHA-256-manifested JSONL export as a model for shipping session history between machines or into a repo without making git the live store.

multi_writer_and_merge:
On one host, several agents can share a store. Every mutation takes a per-file advisory lock (fcntl.flock on POSIX, msvcrt byte lock on Windows with a 30 s timeout), writes are atomic with fsync, and the event log is sharded. The project's swarm benchmark (12 cores, 2026-07-18) measured about 108 ops/s with 1 agent, peaking around 318 ops/s at 12 agents, then falling to 198 ops/s at 24 agents with p99 739 ms, and found no corruption. The project's own assessment says these properties are missing:
- non-duplication: concurrent agents create N copies of the same fact;
- disjoint merge: concurrent edits to one memory are last-writer-wins-or-reject;
- cross-host convergence: none.
Source: https://github.com/0Mattias/bettermemory/blob/main/docs/swarm-convergence-plan.md. The latest commit adds a compare-and-set so that write_note overwrites only the revision the caller read. That claim could not be checked: it shows up in a log line from a neighbouring clone and may belong to another project.

Across hosts, `bettermemory sync push|pull` runs git add -A, commit, `git pull --rebase --no-tags` and push under a store-wide lock. Sync conflicts are plain git merge conflicts with no automatic resolution (https://github.com/0Mattias/bettermemory/blob/main/docs/internals.md#limitations). Since 6.6.0 a pull counts as an admission. Each pulled file goes through these checks in order: a 1 MiB size cap, the store's parser, an id-alias check (a pulled file cannot shadow an existing id), and the credential gate. A refused file stays in git untouched but is quarantined in a gitignored sidecar, so it is never indexed or served. Multi-user use is explicitly out of scope.

search_retrieval:
Ranking is deterministic and lexical only: keyword plus BM25, fused with Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF). It handles plurals and can tokenize CJK, and an optional rescue-expansion vocabulary is off by default. No embeddings are used: they were removed in 4.0, re-added in 5.5, then removed again in 6.0. An FTS5 prefilter kicks in above about 500 memories. Results are scoped automatically to the caller's repo and worktree, and cross-project queries must be explicit. Each hit carries a staleness verdict built from:
- calendar age;
- path drift (cited files that are missing);
- commit drift (commits in verified_head..HEAD that touch the memory's anchors, found with git log --name-only);
- the provenance label;
- superseded_by and contradicts links.
Retrieval is mostly pull-based (memory_search and memory_show). The discovery note's "no auto-injection" is not quite right. Since 3.41.0 (2026-08-05), a score-gated UserPromptSubmit hook injects one id-plus-snippet pointer, never a body, when the silent-miss probe fires. It is on by default and can be disabled with prompt_recall=false. An optional SessionStart "standing tier" (off by default) injects whole bodies, but only for memories labelled local whose verdict is fresh; every other memory appears as a pointer only. There are 27 MCP tools, 18 of them on by default, and the tools/list payload measured 33,960 bytes (https://github.com/0Mattias/bettermemory/blob/main/docs/internals.md, https://github.com/0Mattias/bettermemory/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md).

what_is_stored:
It stores full copies of Claude Code's own local session data, one bundle per session per push. The copy includes:
(1) every raw JSONL record of the session file ~/.claude/projects/<encoded-dir>/<sessionId>.jsonl (user and assistant messages, tool calls and results, as Claude Code wrote them);
(2) the sessions-index.json entry, or a minimal one built by scanning (firstPrompt, messageCount, created/modified, projectPath);
(3) every file under ~/.claude/file-history/<sessionId>/, which are Claude Code's per-edit file snapshots, so source file contents travel with the session (read as text with errors='replace', so binary files are lossy);
(4) the todo list from ~/.claude/todos/<id>-agent-<id>.json.
Sources: exporter.py https://github.com/Daniel-de-Oliveira-Trindade/claude-context-sync/blob/df88be89cf773c5a7b7de96daab87785b815abdf/src/exporter.py and the README's "Bundle Format (v1.1.0)" section https://github.com/Daniel-de-Oliveira-Trindade/claude-context-sync/blob/df88be89cf773c5a7b7de96daab87785b815abdf/README.md.
Nothing is summarized or distilled; it is a verbatim transport of the session. Besides bundles, it stores the device path mappings in config/path_mappings.json, under the tool's own install directory (path_transformer.py). Under ~/.claude-context-sync it keeps an XOR-obfuscated passphrase, a salt, server.json (server URL and raw token) and logs (crypto.py, server_client.py).

scale:
The project makes no scale claims, and I could not verify any usage figures; only an npm downloads badge exists. The practical limits come from GitHub:
- issue comment size (about 65,536 characters; long responses are not truncated by the tool, so oversized comments would presumably fail);
- API rate limits;
- one gh subprocess and one or more GraphQL calls per hook event.

Sources: https://github.com/ej31/claude-session-tracker/blob/main/README.md and https://github.com/ej31/claude-session-tracker/blob/main/hooks/cst_github_utils.py

lesson_for_parinn:
Share history as inert, structured, signed data. Never share it as rendered or runnable content. Never let a convenience feature quietly move key material to the server.

claudereview uploads a pre-rendered HTML page and runs its scripts in the viewer's origin. That makes a shared session an active-content injection channel, the browser version of what ParleyInn's I2 forbids. Its dashboard features also undid its "E2E" claim: the stored `ownerKey`, the server-side re-encryption, and the plaintext titles.

The fragment-key capability URL is still worth borrowing for an opt-in share or transport path. The relay or forge sees only ciphertext plus a salt. But for ParleyInn:
- encrypt the hash-chained event records themselves, not a view of them;
- keep all metadata, titles included, inside the ciphertext;
- render and envelope only on the reading side;
- bind each event to the commit at the moment it is recorded, not to HEAD when it is shared;
- make any server-held key an explicit, audited opt-in (I6).

Artifacts confirms two ParleyInn choices and shows a third gap. First, it gives each session its own isolated history with its own single writer, and links a separate metadata layer to code through git notes. That is close to ParleyInn's per-session append-only chain, and it suggests ParleyInn could export or transport a session as git notes or a ref without making git the store of record. Second, a small dependency-free git smart-HTTP engine is enough to make any store look like a git remote, so git can be just a transport and the storage can stay ParleyInn's own (SQLite). The gap: Artifacts publishes nothing on encryption, redaction, or treating recalled notes as untrusted, and it puts bearer tokens in remote URLs. If ParleyInn ever syncs through a hosted git remote like this, the guarantees have to come from ParleyInn itself:
- seal or encrypt records before push;
- keep credentials out of URLs and transcripts;
- verify the hash chain on fetch;
- envelope anything pulled back (I2).

The core also still has to make no network calls of its own (I4), so any sync has to live in a separate, opt-in process.

Cognee shows why the lossless record should be the authority and the graph a rebuildable view of it. In Cognee the transcript is the throwaway layer. The session cache expires after 7 days by default, its QA rows can be updated, and its contents are distilled by non-deterministic LLM extraction into a graph that cannot be rebuilt exactly. The hash-chained ledger exists but is opt-in, is not mirrored for updates and deletes, and silently swallows its own errors, which is the opposite of ParleyInn's I6 and I10. Cognee also recalls memory automatically into every prompt, an automatic path from untrusted content to the model of the kind ParleyInn's I2 forbids. ParleyInn should invert all three:
- keep an append-only, hash-chained event log as the only source of truth;
- keep recall pull-only and enveloped;
- treat any graph or vector index as a disposable projection.

The Kuzu archival is a second lesson. It forced Cognee to rename its backend and ship an on-disk storage-code migrator (ladybug_migrate, pinned per version in pyproject.toml). ParleyInn's durable record should therefore be a simple, self-describing format that does not depend on any one embedded engine's storage layout.

search_retrieval:
There is no full-text or semantic search over session history. Retrieval works in three ways:
- offset-based replay of a stream (catch-up reads can be CDN-cached);
- reactive, typed live queries over materialised collections through TanStack DB or StreamDB (https://electric.ax/docs/streams/durable-state.md);
- Postgres WHERE-clause Shapes for synced tables (https://electric.ax/docs/sync/guides/shapes.md).

Agent context is assembled with `ctx.useContext()` from "token-budgeted sources" with cache tiers, and context insertions and removals are logged as events (https://electric.ax/llms.txt, https://electric.ax/docs/agents/reference/built-in-collections.md). Any search index would have to be built by the user as a projection.

lesson_for_parinn:
Durable Streams is the clearest prior art for a real-time transport that ParleyInn could adopt without running its core on the network. A session is one append-only, offset-addressed stream. Fork is a first-class "new stream from offset X" and is never merged. Multi-writer safety comes from Kafka-style idempotent producers (producer id, epoch and sequence, with stale-epoch fencing), not from merge logic. Readers resume by offset over plain HTTP, with SSE and CDN caching.

ParleyInn could mirror this design in a separate, optional relay or sidecar process:
- give each session's hash-chained record a monotonic offset;
- give each writer (agent, worktree, sandbox) its own epoch so a zombie sandbox is fenced;
- treat forks as new chains that point back to a parent offset and are never merged.

ParleyInn should also note Electric's gaps, because they are ParleyInn's differentiators:
- auth is out of scope at the protocol layer;
- there is no hash chain or tamper evidence;
- there is no redaction or encryption at rest;
- the agent's full transcript, including reasoning and tool output, is replayed straight back into model context.

Electric's own spec says stream contents MUST be treated as untrusted. That matches ParleyInn's I2, but Electric's agent runtime then uses the stream as the context source. ParleyInn should keep the pull-only, enveloped recall boundary even if it adopts a Durable-Streams-compatible wire format for transport.

what_is_stored:
It stores curated "thoughts", not raw sessions, prompts or tool transcripts. A thought is a short claim that an agent saves explicitly through MCP (save_thought, learn_preference, supersede, change_scope). Each one has a source_type: observation, inference, user_input, tool_output or decision. Each also carries a lifecycle status: draft, proposed, approved, rejected, error or tombstoned. The docs frame FAVA as "the governed institutional record for decisions, observations, validation, and lineage. It is not the operational working-context store" (https://github.com/MachineWisdomAI/fava-trails/blob/main/docs/governed-recall.md). Other things stored alongside thoughts:
- typed relationships between thoughts;
- supersession lineage;
- approval provenance in metadata.extra.approval (kind llm_advisory or human, actor, timestamp; for the decisions policy also the Noul probability and threshold);
- a per-scope Trust Gate prompt (trails/trust-gate-prompt.md);
- config.yaml, which holds the remote, push_strategy, hooks and Trust Gate settings.
Sources: https://github.com/MachineWisdomAI/fava-trails/blob/main/src/fava_trails/models.py, https://github.com/MachineWisdomAI/fava-trails/blob/main/src/fava_trails/data_repo_template/README.md

format_and_model:
**File format.** One Markdown file per thought, named <ULID>.md, with a YAML frontmatter header (https://github.com/MachineWisdomAI/fava-trails/blob/main/src/fava_trails/models.py). Frontmatter fields:
- schema_version (1), thought_id (a ULID), parent_id, agent_id, confidence (0–1), source_type, validation_status, intent_ref, created_at;
- supersedes_id / supersedes_scope and superseded_by / superseded_scope;
- relationships: a list of {type, target_id}, where type is DEPENDS_ON, REVISED_BY, AUTHORED_BY, REFERENCES or SUPERSEDES;
- metadata: project, branch, tags and a free-form extra dict.

**Layout.** trails/<org>/<team>/<project>/thoughts/{drafts, observations, decisions, intents, preferences/{firm,client}}/<ULID>.md. Scopes are slash paths. On promotion, source_type picks the namespace: decision goes to decisions/; observation, inference and tool_output go to observations/; user_input goes to preferences/ (https://github.com/MachineWisdomAI/fava-trails/blob/main/src/fava_trails/data_repo_template/README.md).

**Mutability.** The store is not append-only:
- drafts can be rewritten in place with update_thought;
- promotion deletes the draft path and writes the file into the permanent namespace;
- approving a successor writes superseded_by into the predecessor's frontmatter;
- once a thought is approved, rejected, tombstoned or superseded, its body is frozen, but its metadata can still change.
Sources: https://github.com/MachineWisdomAI/fava-trails/blob/main/docs/fava_trails_faq.md, https://github.com/MachineWisdomAI/fava-trails/blob/main/src/fava_trails/trail.py

**Commits.** Every write becomes one JJ commit with a templated message such as "Save thought <id8> [<type>] in <ns>/" or "Review <id8>: approved in decisions/". All commits are authored as "FAVA Trail <fava@machine-wisdom.ai>", so the agent's identity lives only in frontmatter (https://github.com/MachineWisdomAI/fava-trails/blob/main/src/fava_trails/vcs/jj_backend.py).

**Integrity.** There is no application-level hash chain or signing. Integrity comes only from git and JJ object hashes. Agents never see raw jj output, because a "semantic translation layer" returns structured JSON instead (https://github.com/MachineWisdomAI/fava-trails/blob/main/docs/fava_trails_faq.md).

what_is_stored:
One small JSON "provenance record" per commit (or per PR). It holds: target (commit SHA or PR number); attribution.by (human | copilot | pair | unknown | agent:<name>) plus an RFC 3339 timestamp; intent (a one-line summary, an optional spec reference, an 8/16-hex spec_hash, and an origin enum: human/spec/prompt/template/upstream/ai/unknown); and context (ticket, prompt, model, branch). The data comes from:
- the commit subject, parsed as a conventional commit (feat maps to origin spec, chore to human);
- a [A-Z]+-\d+ ticket pattern in the branch name;
- environment variables at commit time. AI_AGENT is parsed to get the agent name. The model comes from the first of CLAUDE_CODE_MODEL, COPILOT_AGENT_MODEL, COPILOT_MODEL, ANTHROPIC_MODEL, OPENAI_MODEL, CLAUDE_MODEL, GITHUB_MODEL, AI_MODEL, GITWHY_MODEL, GHW_MODEL. The prompt comes from COPILOT_AGENT_PROMPT, GITWHY_PROMPT or GHW_PROMPT;
- a hand-edited .gitwhy/session.yaml (model, prompt, ticket, origin);
- optionally, an LLM-written summary from an external CLI fed the changed file names or the full diff.

It does NOT store transcripts, tool calls, multiple prompts or session ids. The README says $CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT is captured, but the code at HEAD does not read it (cmd/commit.go envPromptVars). Older notes in the project's own refs/notes/gitwhy also carry files and diff_stats fields that the current schema no longer has.

lesson_for_parinn:
If ParleyInn ever uses git notes as transport, it must not use a single force-written notes ref with a forced (+) fetch refspec. gitwhy shows that pattern silently drops other writers' records, and that mutable notes defeat audit claims. ParleyInn would need:
- per-writer namespaces, for example refs/notes/parinn/<agent-or-machine-id>, or a custom ref per writer, so writes never conflict;
- an append-only hash-chained payload, so overwrites and tampering are detectable;
- a non-forced fetch into a staging ref followed by a verify-then-merge step.

Separately, commit-time environment variables are a cheap, offline way to join a session to its commits. ParleyInn could record its own session id in a commit trailer or note from a hook, rather than duplicate transcripts into git. Anything read back from notes stays untrusted.

what_is_stored:
Generic key-to-bytes pairs that satisfy the go-datastore Datastore and Batching interfaces. Each Put or Delete becomes a 'delta': a protobuf with elements {key, id, value}, tombstones, a uint64 priority and, since 2026, a dagName (pb/delta.proto). The delta is wrapped in an IPLD node that links to the current heads. The library also keeps derived local state:
- per-key element and tombstone markers, indexed by block (/set/s/<key>/<block>, /set/t/<key>/<block>);
- the winning value and priority for each key (/set/k/<key>/v and /p);
- the current heads with their heights (h namespace) and the heads of each named DAG (a namespace);
- markers for processed blocks (b), a dirty bit, a bad-shutdown key and a version key (crdt.go, set.go).

Deleting a key only adds a tombstone. The package doc says the store "will only grow in size even when keys are deleted" unless it is compacted manually. Its production user is IPFS Cluster, where the stored data is the cluster pinset.

scale:
sum.golang.org's tree held 66,338,570 records when fetched on 2026-10-02 (https://sum.golang.org/latest).
Design claims from research.swtch.com/tlog (March 2019):
- hash storage is about 2N hashes, so about 64 bytes per record at 32-byte SHA-256;
- record and consistency proofs are O(lg N) hashes, about 26 hashes or 832 bytes at N≈66M (my own arithmetic);
- a height-8 tile is 8 kB, and a proof in a 100M-record log needs about three tiles, 24 kB.
`Tile.N` is unbounded int64 and the level is at most 63. The package itself makes no throughput claims.

what_is_stored:
It stores LLM-distilled summaries, not transcripts. The README says each memory holds a "reasoning trace" (CONCLUSION/INSIGHT pairs), key decisions (choice and reason), files touched and constraints. The landing page says "No code storage — We store reasoning and metadata, never your actual source code" (README; landing/src/pages/limitations.astro). The local SQLite file also holds:
- per-session drift-detection state: original goal, raw_user_prompt, expected scope, keywords, drift scores, pending corrections, final_response;
- a step log of tool actions (edit, bash, read and so on), with files, the command and reasoning;
- a file_reasoning table (file_path, anchor, line_start/line_end, code_hash, reasoning);
- a drift_log of rejected actions (src/core/store/database.ts).
On the server, a memory also carries evolution_steps (up to 10), reasoning_evolution (up to 5 condensed earlier versions) and decisions with active and superseded_by flags (up to 20). Older entries are cut off, so the history is deliberately lossy (src/core/cloud/cloud-sync.ts, prepareSyncPayload, MAX_DECISIONS=20, MAX_EVOLUTION_STEPS=10, MAX_REASONING_EVOLUTION=5). The server also keeps per-chunk embeddings in memory_chunks; chunk types are summary, query, goal, reasoning and decision (api/src/lib/embeddings.ts). Source: https://github.com/TonyStef/Grov/blob/main/src/core/store/database.ts

format_and_model:
Relational, mutable rows. In SQLite:
- tasks: id, project_path, user, original_query, goal, summary, system_name, a status enum (complete|question|partial|abandoned), trigger_reason (complete|threshold|abandoned), linked_commit, parent_task_id, synced_at, sync_error;
- reasoning_trace, files_touched, decisions, constraints and tags are stored as JSON text columns;
- session_states, steps, file_reasoning and drift_log, as listed above.
Schema migrations run as ad-hoc ALTER TABLE statements in try/catch on every start. On the server, a Memory row (shared/src/types/memory.ts) adds team_id, user_id, client_task_id, branch, source_branch, merged_at, evolution_steps and reasoning_evolution. memory_chunks holds 1536-dimension text-embedding-3-small vectors per chunk. A memory is edited in place, with no event log and no hashing. Sources: https://github.com/TonyStef/Grov/blob/main/shared/src/types/memory.ts and https://github.com/TonyStef/Grov/blob/main/src/core/store/database.ts

scale:
There are no throughput or storage scale claims. Marketing claims: "Tasks drop from 10+ minutes to 1-2 minutes when team context is available", and "Free for individuals and teams up to 3 developers" (README). Built-in limits:
- 3 memories injected per prompt;
- sync batches of 10;
- caps of 20 decisions, 10 evolution steps and 5 reasoning-evolution entries per memory;
- embedding input cut at 30,000 characters;
- per-route API rate limits, e.g. 5 branch merges a minute (api/src/routes/branches.ts; api/src/lib/embeddings.ts).
None of this was independently verified.

format_and_model:
Upload unit = one session snapshot: the main transcript content plus up to 512 sub-agent files ({agentId, content}). Limits: 128 MiB aggregate, 160M UTF-16 code units per content field (contracts/ingest.ts). Old ClickHouse model: one row per session. `content` is a single String holding the whole JSONL and `subagents` is Map(String,String). Engine ReplacingMergeTree(ingested_at), ORDER BY (organization_id, session_date, session_id), partitioned monthly, TTL 365 days (base-sessions.ts at e2e5884). Evidence model: content-addressed objects with id `sha256:<hex>`. Object kinds are context-manifest, source-blob, git-diff, transcript-chunk, ci-metadata and ci-log, each up to 64 MiB, at most 4,096 objects and 128 MiB per delivery. Each capture carries contextId, a parentContextId (chain), baseGitCommit/headGitCommit, lifecycle (start/resume/checkpoint/end) and a transcript watermark {byteOffset, eventOrdinal, lastEventAt}. Per-area coverage is marked complete/partial/unavailable and partial or unavailable requires an explicit reason (contracts/repository-evidence.ts). Transcript revision manifest (lib/transcript-revision.ts, version 1):
- scope {actorId, provider, providerInstanceId, sessionId}
- an append-only list of chunk refs {startByte, endByte, recordCount, sha256}
- watermark {byteOffset, prefixSha256, recordCount, lastOrdinal}
- parentRevisionId, generation and terminal
- revisionId = SHA-256 of the canonical JSON of all the above, so revisions form a hash chain
Only whole JSONL lines are taken (the stable end is the last newline). The generation increments and the chain restarts when the scope changes, the source shrinks, or the prefix hash no longer matches. An attribution manifest ('opaline-capture-attribution/v1') ties each root or child stream's byte-range prefix and sha256 to native ids: Claude agentId, Codex thread and turn ids.

what_is_stored:
(a) The environment definition: chosen GitHub repositories, an "Install script", a "Start skill" that starts services and checks they are ready, environment variables, network secrets with their allowed domains, the internet-access policy, VPN (Tailscale) settings, OIDC attachments, and privacy ("Who can use"). Publishing "captures the prepared filesystem for new tasks". Saving only stores configuration (https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/environments/cloud-environments).
(b) Per-task workspace state: a task "continues with its own saved files, including uncommitted changes and installed tools". By default the saved VM state "is recoverable for up to seven days after you last start a turn or resume the task". The docs warn: "Saved state doesn't replace source control" (same page).
(c) The task or chat record on OpenAI's backend. The open-source CLI client reads these fields:
- task id, title, status (pending/ready/applied/error), updated_at, environment_id and label;
- a diff summary (files changed, lines added, lines removed), is_review, and attempt_total (best-of-N);
- turns, each with input_items (user messages, plus an optional `pre_apply_patch` diff) and output_items (assistant messages and `output_diff` unified diffs);
- per turn: a `worklog` of messages with author role and content parts, turn_status, sibling_turn_ids, attempt_placement, and an error code/message;
- task-level `archived` and `external_pull_requests` fields.
Sources: https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/cloud-tasks-client/src/api.rs, https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/backend-client/src/types.rs, https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/backend-client/tests/fixtures/task_details_with_diff.json (main at commit 14a477ea, 2026-10-02).
(d) Legacy only: cached container state "for up to 12 hours". It is shared across all users of the environment on Business and Enterprise plans (https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/environments/cloud-environment).
(e) Enterprise compliance records: an "append-only compliance log stream" downloaded as JSONL. A `codex_log` event type is documented, but only for Work local access. On the Work Cloud security page, Compliance Logs Platform records "are available for 30 days" (https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/enterprise/compliance-api, https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/enterprise/chatgpt-work-cloud-security).
Not verified: how long task transcripts and worklogs themselves are kept, and exactly which Codex cloud events the compliance stream covers. The docs defer to the Admin API reference (https://chatgpt.com/public/admin/api-reference), which was not read.

Everything that makes up a plan, versioned per event. Per the docs, these events create a new version: adding, removing or updating context; sending a prompt; Plandex's reply; building proposed file updates into pending changes; rejecting pending changes; applying them; and changing models or model settings (https://github.com/plandex-ai/plandex/blob/main/docs/docs/core-concepts/version-control.md). Concretely, the server keeps:
- every conversation message in full (role, text, token count, message number, subtasks, flags, userId);
- context items, including full bodies of loaded project files, URLs and images, plus tree-sitter map parts and per-file SHAs;
- per-file plan results (path, full content or replacements, appliedAt/rejectedAt, syntax errors);
- per-message descriptions (commit message, operations, build state);
- apply records (which messages and results were applied, by which user).

Sources: https://github.com/plandex-ai/plandex/blob/main/app/server/db/data_models.go and https://github.com/plandex-ai/plandex/blob/main/app/server/db/fs.go.

Rolling conversation summaries are stored separately. They replace older messages in the model's context when the token limit is hit, but the full conversation is kept (https://github.com/plandex-ai/plandex/blob/main/docs/docs/core-concepts/conversations.md). Model API keys are not stored (see privacy_security).

Records: opaque bytes on unbounded, totally ordered, append-only streams. Each record has (https://s2.dev/docs/concepts/records):
- a sequence number that S2 assigns and that strictly increases;
- a millisecond timestamp, from arrival or from the client (`client-prefer` by default);
- byte-valued headers (name/value pairs);
- a byte body.

A record can be at most 1 MiB in metered bytes. Metered size is 8 bytes + 2 per header + header bytes + body (https://s2.dev/docs/limits).

S2 does not interpret payloads, with one exception: "command records". These carry a single header with an empty name, and the operations are `fence` and `trim` (https://s2.dev/docs/concepts/command-records.md).

For agents, the docs recommend one stream per agent run, `s2://my-basin/agents/{run-id}`, holding "an ordered log of everything that happened — tool calls, LLM responses, state changes" (https://s2.dev/docs/use-cases/agents.md). The Jul 2025 blog post goes further: "user inputs, prompts, model responses, and tool calls" all become events on the session stream (https://s2.dev/blog/agent-sessions).

The Anthropic integration (`@s2-dev/resumable-stream/anthropic`) stores raw Anthropic Messages API stream events and replays them as SSE. It does not build a transcript itself: "does not infer user messages" (https://s2.dev/docs/integrations/anthropic).

format_and_model:
Resource hierarchy: account > basin > stream > record. A basin is a globally named namespace, "similar to a bucket" (https://s2.dev/docs/llms.txt). The docs say basins, streams and access tokens are "unlimited in cardinality".

Stream positions:
- head: the first retained record;
- tail: `(next_seq_num, last_record_timestamp)` (https://s2.dev/docs/concepts/streams.md).

Stream config (https://s2.dev/docs/concepts/configs.md):
- storage_class;
- retention_policy: age-based or infinite, **default 7 days**;
- delete_on_empty;
- timestamping mode. Timestamps are forced to be monotonic, and client timestamps are capped at arrival time by default.

Basin config: create_stream_on_append / create_stream_on_read, plus stream_cipher.

Wire formats:
- REST/JSON, where bodies are raw UTF-8 or `s2-format: base64`;
- protobuf;
- S2S, a binary session protocol over HTTP/2.

The OpenAPI spec is generated from s2-lite (https://s2.dev/docs/s2-lite.md).

Compaction: trimming (explicit `trim` command records) and snapshot-and-follow patterns, with snapshots stored outside the stream or inside it (https://s2.dev/docs/concepts/snapshots.md, https://s2.dev/docs/concepts/trimming.md).

S2 has no built-in schema, content addressing or hash chain.

scale:
S2 claims (https://s2.dev/docs/limits, https://s2.dev/docs/platform/architecture.md, https://s2.dev/docs/s2-lite.md):
- an unlimited number of streams and basins (free tier: 100 basins and at most 28 days of retention);
- appends up to 100 MiB/s per stream and up to 200 batches/s per stream per connection;
- read limits "TBD", described as elastic, with read-through caching through cachey (open source) that acts "effectively a CDN for streams";
- streams can migrate between channels to balance load.

s2-lite runs on a single node that scales vertically, and read fan-out is limited to what one node can serve.

Pricing (https://s2.dev/pricing.md):
- $0.05 per GiB-month retained;
- $0.01 per 1000 stream-months;
- writes: $0.05/GiB (Standard) and $0.075/GiB (Express);
- reads: $0.10/GiB over the internet.

So one stream per session is cheap.

